{"event":"changes_requested","job_id":"docs-point-of-sale-mobile-ios-understand","reason":"Full pinned raw independently read; SHA-256 and all five quote locators match. The candidate answers both fixed queries and preserves the iOS Mobile POS session, PIN model/version, reader firmware, and Bluetooth-versus-USB-dock boundaries. Both proposed reciprocal source links have valid targets. One material conflict remains in wiki/concepts/adyen-mobile-card-readers.md: its unqualified Definition says USB-C requires an Android device with USB host mode, while this iOS source (raw lines 137\u2013143) explicitly describes an iOS mobile-device USB-C connection through the NYC1 dock. The older NYC1 guide's Android-only condition applies to a direct cable from device to reader (raw NYC1 manual line 68). For the query 'Can an iOS Mobile SDK app connect to NYC1 through a USB-C dock?', the current concept can lead to the wrong answer; a reciprocal link alone does not qualify that retained sentence."}
